  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## Basic word order in English: Grammar chart\n\n![Grammar chart illustrating the basic word order in English sentences, placement of adverbs of frequency, and tips to avoid common mistakes.](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-1.png)\n\n### Subject + verb\n\nIn English, we normally need to include the subject in a sentence. The subject goes before the verb.\n\n-   ****We** dance.**\n-   ****She** is waiting.**\n\nWe use **it** or **they** as a subject to talk about something that we mentioned before.\n\n-   *I like your guitar. **Is** nice. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-2.png)* \n-   *I like your guitar. **It** is nice.* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-3.png)*\n-   *Can you see those trees? **Are** beautiful. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-4.png)* \n-   *Can you see those trees? **They** are beautiful.* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-5.png)*\n\nWe don’t include the subject in [imperative sentences](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/imperative-sit-dont-talk/).\n\n-   *Sit down, please.*\n-   *Call me soon.*\n\nWe can put an [adverb of frequency](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/adverbs-frequency/) between the subject and the verb. BUT adverbs of frequency go after the verb when the verb is [**be**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-simple-forms-of-to-be/).\n\n-   *We **often dance**.*\n-   BUT: *You **are always** late.*\n\n### Verb + object\n\nWhen there is an object in the sentence, we need to put it after the verb. Verb and object are together.\n\n-   *I **like** **very much** pizza. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-6.png)* \n-   *I **like pizza** very much.* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-7.png)* \n-   *She **plays** **every day** the guitar. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-8.png)* \n-   *She **plays the guitar** every day.* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-9.png)* \n\n### Place + time\n\nWe normally say ‘where’ and ‘when’ something happens in this order.\n\n-   *She arrives **in the morning at the hospital**. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-10.png)* \n-   *She arrives **at the hospital in the morning**. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-11.png)* \n-   *Will you be **at 3 pm** **in the office**? ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-12.png)* \n-   *Will you be **in the office at 3 pm**? ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/basic-word-order-in-english/img-13.png)*",
